by GromNaN at 2026-08-18T18:10:36Z

I tested this bridge on the Symfony Demo application (linked against this PR branch) with a real MongoDB replica set, and it works very well.

Setup: created a simple `SendReminder` message and handler, routed it to a MongoDB transport, and configured a dead letter (failure) transport on the same collection with a dedicated `queue_name`. The transport factory was already registered by FrameworkBundle, so no manual wiring was needed. `messenger:setup-transports` created the collection and the compound index, the nominal dispatch/consume/ack flow worked, and failing messages were retried and correctly moved to the failure transport. I also confirmed it works with both the default PHP serializer and the JSON serializer.

Config used:

```yaml
# config/packages/messenger.yaml
framework:
    messenger:
        failure_transport: failed

        # Optional: store the body as JSON instead of PHP-serialized data
        serializer:
            default_serializer: messenger.transport.symfony_serializer

        transports:
            async:
                dsn: '%env(MONGODB_URI)%&collection_name=messenger_messages&queue_name=default'
                retry_strategy:
                    max_retries: 3
                    delay: 1000

            failed:
                dsn: '%env(MONGODB_URI)%&collection_name=messenger_messages&queue_name=failed'

        routing:
            App\Message\SendReminder: async
```

```dotenv
# .env
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017,127.0.0.1:27018,127.0.0.1:27019/symfony_demo_messenger?replicaSet=rs0
```

The DSN being a real MongoDB URI (hosts and database in the path, transport options appended to the query string) is convenient.

One suggestion: even with the JSON serializer, the message body is stored as a JSON string. A BSON serialization option that stores the decoded payload as a native sub-document could be relevant here, since it would make the payload queryable and indexable directly in MongoDB. Nice work!

